\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Two runtime checker roles provide evidence at different stages. Separate prompts avoid direct reuse of Adapter or model code, but shared LLM and schema errors remain possible. The case-specific acceptance judge uses held-out truth and is not provided to the runtime agents.}}{1}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:verificationlayers}{{1}{1}{Two runtime checker roles provide evidence at different stages. Separate prompts avoid direct reuse of Adapter or model code, but shared LLM and schema errors remain possible. The case-specific acceptance judge uses held-out truth and is not provided to the runtime agents}{figure.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Evidence routes failures to the relevant stage. A repaired version must pass checks before activation. Proven infeasibility avoids an annealer submission; a timed-out preflight does not prove infeasibility. A validated baseline may be delivered when annealing fails or trails it. Repairs and solver calls are bounded.}}{2}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:recoveryroutes}{{2}{2}{Evidence routes failures to the relevant stage. A repaired version must pass checks before activation. Proven infeasibility avoids an annealer submission; a timed-out preflight does not prove infeasibility. A validated baseline may be delivered when annealing fails or trails it. Repairs and solver calls are bounded}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Design of the autonomous acceptance comparison, not completed experimental results. Five development families each have two scales. Filled markers denote enabled mechanisms; hollow markers denote disabled mechanisms. The direct baseline uses different tools and download limits, so its comparison with the pipeline cannot isolate agent count. Joint success requires all three acceptance outcomes.}}{3}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:acceptancedesign}{{3}{3}{Design of the autonomous acceptance comparison, not completed experimental results. Five development families each have two scales. Filled markers denote enabled mechanisms; hollow markers denote disabled mechanisms. The direct baseline uses different tools and download limits, so its comparison with the pipeline cannot isolate agent count. Joint success requires all three acceptance outcomes}{figure.3}{}}
